.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::Process 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::Process
.Nd child process management
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::Process;

my $result = Fugu::Process->spawn_command(
    cmd       => [ '/usr/local/bin/mydaemon', '-f' ],
    daemonize => 1,
    stdout    => '/var/log/mydaemon.log',
);

Fugu::Process->terminate($result->{pid}) if $result->{success};

my $r = Fugu::Process->run(cmd => [ 'rcctl', 'check', 'mydaemon' ]);
print $r->{stdout} if $r->{success};
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
does the parts of child-process control that are easy to get wrong.
It shows the difference between a child that runs and a child that
exits immediately.
It sends
.Dv SIGTERM
first and sends
.Dv SIGKILL
after a delay.
It reaps zombies and does not block.
.Pp
Two methods start a child.
.Fn spawn_command
leaves it running.
.Fn run
waits for it and captures what it wrote.
Both report a failed
.Xr execve 2
exactly, over a close-on-exec pipe, and never by a wait-and-guess
.Xr sleep 3 .
Neither one runs a shell: the command is a list, so no argument needs
quoting and no argument can become a shell operator.
.Pp
The module keeps no state.
Every method is a class method.
.Ss spawn_command
.Fn spawn_command "%args"
forks,
redirects the standard descriptors,
and runs a command.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "check_aliveXX"
.It Fa cmd
An array reference that holds the command and its arguments.
This argument is necessary and must not be empty.
.It Fa daemonize
If this argument is true, the child calls
.Xr setsid 2
before exec.
The default is false.
.It Fa stdin , Fa stdout , Fa stderr
The paths for the child's standard descriptors.
The default for each path is
.Pa /dev/null .
.It Fa check_alive
The number of seconds to wait before the check that the child continues
to run.
The default is 0, which skips the check.
The
.Xr execve 2
outcome does not need this wait: the method always has it before it
returns.
.It Fa on_success
A code reference that the method calls with the child's process ID when
the spawn is successful.
.It Fa on_error
A code reference that the method calls with a message when the spawn
fails.
.El
.Pp
The parent always waits for the
.Xr execve 2
to resolve.
The child holds the write end of a close-on-exec pipe.
A successful
.Xr execve 2
closes that end, and the parent reads end-of-file.
A failure leaves a message in the pipe, and the parent returns it in
.Fa error .
Thus a command that does not exist reports its own reason at once.
.Pp
.Fa check_alive
answers a different question:
.Em is the child still there after N seconds .
When it is not zero, the parent waits that long and then does two
tests.
A
.Xr waitpid 2
call that does not block reaps the child and shows that it exited.
A
.Xr kill 2
call with signal 0 shows that the child is still there.
.Pp
A child that exits at once with status 0 is not a failure.
The result carries
.Fa success
set to 1,
.Fa exited
set to 1 and
.Fa exit_code
set to 0.
Thus a caller that expects a long-lived child can tell the two apart.
.Ss run
.Fn run "%args"
runs a command to completion and captures what it wrote.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "timeoutXX"
.It Fa cmd
An array reference that holds the command and its arguments.
This argument is necessary and must not be empty.
.It Fa timeout
The number of seconds to wait before the method stops the child.
The default is no limit.
.It Fa stdin
A string to feed to the child on its standard input.
.It Fa cwd
A directory to run the child in.
The child calls
.Xr chdir 2
after the fork and before the
.Xr execve 2 ,
so the working directory of the caller does not change.
A
.Xr chdir 2
in the caller would change the meaning of every other relative path in
the program,
and a second call that ran at the same time would race it.
.Pp
A directory that the child cannot enter is a startup failure with the
reason in
.Va error ,
not a silent run in the wrong place.
.It Fa passthrough
Let the child write straight to the caller's terminal.
.Va stdout
and
.Va stderr
then come back empty.
.El
.Pp
The method reads standard output and standard error at the same time.
A reader that took them in sequence would deadlock: a child that fills
one pipe blocks until someone drains it.
.Ss exit_code
.Fn exit_code "$status"
maps a raw
.Xr waitpid 2
status, or the return value of
.Ic system ,
to an exit code between 0 and 255.
The low byte holds the terminating signal.
The high byte holds the exit code.
A value of \-1 means the child never started.
.Pp
A caller that gives a raw status to
.Ic exit
turns a remote exit code of 1 into
.Ql exit(256) ,
which the kernel truncates to 0.
That silently reports a failed command as a success.
.Ss is_alive
.Fn is_alive "$pid"
reports if a process exists and is not a zombie.
The check reaps a zombie child as a side effect and then reports it as
not alive.
A caller that needs the exit status uses
.Fn run ,
or waits itself.
.Ss terminate
.Fn terminate "$pid" "%args"
sends
.Dv SIGTERM ,
waits,
and sends
.Dv SIGKILL
if the process continues to run.
.Pp
These are the arguments:
.Bl -tag -width "grace_periodXX"
.It Fa grace_period
The number of seconds to wait between the two signals.
The default is 5.
.It Fa on_kill
A code reference that the method calls when the process is gone.
.El
.Pp
The wait polls with sub-second granularity.
Thus a child that stops at once does not cost a whole second.
.Ss reap
.Fn reap "$pid"
reaps one zombie child and does not block.
.Ss reap_all
.Fn reap_all
reaps each zombie child that is ready and does not block.
It returns the number of children that it reaped.
.Ss wait_exit
.Fn wait_exit "$pid" "$timeout"
polls until the process exits or until the timeout ends.
The default for
.Fa $timeout
is 30 seconds.
.Ss spawn_perl
.Fn spawn_perl "%args"
runs Perl code in a child process.
It gives the child the parent's
.Ic -I
paths.
The parent gets these paths from
.Ic -I ,
.Ic use lib
or
.Ev PERL5LIB .
Thus the child sees the same modules.
.Pp
.Fa code
is the program text.
.Fa args
is an array reference of arguments for the program.
The method gives all other arguments to
.Fn spawn_command .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn spawn_command
and
.Fn spawn_perl
return a hash reference.
On success, the hash holds
.Fa success
set to 1 and
.Fa pid .
On failure,
.Fa success
is 0 and
.Fa error
gives the cause.
When the spawn makes a child and the child exits at once,
the hash also holds
.Fa exited
and
.Fa exit_code .
.Pp
.Fn run
returns a hash reference that holds
.Fa success ,
.Fa stdout ,
.Fa stderr ,
.Fa exit_code
and
.Fa timed_out .
On a failure to start the child, it also holds
.Fa error .
.Fa success
is 1 only when the child exited with code 0 and did not time out.
.Pp
.Fn exit_code
returns a number between 0 and 255.
.Pp
.Fn is_alive
returns 1 or 0.
.Pp
.Fn terminate
returns 1 if the process is gone.
It returns 0 if the process continues after
.Dv SIGKILL .
.Pp
.Fn reap
returns 1 when it reaps the child or when the child does not exist.
It returns 0 when the child continues to run.
.Fn reap_all
returns a count.
.Pp
.Fn wait_exit
returns 1 if the process exits in the timeout period.
If not, it returns 0.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example runs a helper and then stops it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $r = Fugu::Process->spawn_command(
    cmd      => [ 'mdnsctl', 'publish', $name, '_hap', 'tcp', $port ],
    on_error => sub ($err) { $log->err('mdnsctl: %s', $err) },
);

Fugu::Process->terminate($r->{pid}, grace_period => 10)
    if $r->{success};
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
No method dies.
The methods report failures through the hash reference or the boolean
value that they return.
When you give an
.Fa on_error
callback, the methods also report the failure through it.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr setsid 2 ,
.Xr waitpid 2 ,
.Xr Fugu::Log 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Pidfile 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn spawn_command
blocks the caller for
.Fa check_alive
seconds when that argument is not zero.
Thus a program that spawns several children waits the full time for
each child in sequence.
Leave the argument at its default of 0 unless the question it answers
is the one you have.
.Pp
.Fn run
holds the whole output of the child in memory.
Do not use it for a command that writes without a bound.
.Pp
.Fn is_alive
and
.Fn reap
call
.Xr waitpid 2 .
That call reaps only the children of the caller.
For all other processes, they use
.Xr kill 2
with signal 0.
This signal cannot show the difference between a live process and a
zombie.
.Pp
The operating system uses process IDs again for new processes.
The module cannot show the difference between the initial process and a
later process with the same number.
